System-Time: Wed Sep 22 19:05:30 1999
To: boatyard
From: Mark Richardson <mc_richardson@hotmail.com>
Subject: Fwd: Do this Y2K check on your computer
Cc: batikart

For those of you running Windows this is a fix for a small Y2K problem
almost everyone should do.
After running this quick little test, much to my surprise, I learned that my
computer may have failed on 01/01/2000 due to a computer clock setting.
Fortunately, a quick fix is provided, should your computer fail the test.

TEST:

Double click on "My Computer" icon.
Double click on "Control Panel"  icon.
Double click on "Regional Settings" icon.
Click on the date tab at top of the page.

Where it says "Short Date Sample" look and see if it shows
a "two digit" year.
Of course, it does. That's the default  setting for windows 95,
Windows 98 and NT.
This date is the date that feeds application software and WILL NOT
rollover in the year 2000.  It will roll over to 00.

Click on the button across from "Short Date Style" and selecte the
option that shows, mm/dd/yyyy.
(Be sure that your selection has four Y's showing, not just 2).

Now click on Apply and then click on OK at the bottom.

Easy enough to fix.

Every single installation (yy) of Windows Worldwide is defaulted
to fail Y2K rollover.

Please pass this on remembering there are no warranties nor liabilities
attached, real or implied.

